{
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "detail": {
    "days": 14,
    "checkpoint": "Stop once you can independently solve and explain one changed simple series circuit using Ohm's law, including choosing and justifying the correct form of the law."
  },
  "plans": [
    {
      "planRef": "plan_1",
      "blueprint": {
        "schemaVersion": 2,
        "strategy": {
          "policy": "EXAM",
          "outcome": "Explain and use Ohm's law for one simple series circuit. Stop once I can solve and explain one changed circuit independently.",
          "explanation": "The accepted outcome is deliberately small and bounded: explain and use Ohm's law in one simple series circuit, then stop after one independent changed circuit. There is no deadline, no verified assessment weighting, and no observed readiness evidence, so the smallest useful strategy is one integrated activity that teaches the rule, applies it to a worked series circuit, and checks understanding with a changed circuit. Because the learner explicitly asked to stop once they can solve and explain one changed circuit independently, a single committed activity is sufficient; no separate relearning, multiple practice sets, or second check is justified. A contingent remediation activity is included only if the independent changed circuit fails, anchored to the committed checkpoint.",
          "recommendedEffort": {
            "lowMinutes": 25,
            "likelyMinutes": 35,
            "highMinutes": 50
          },
          "phases": []
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The single integrated activity teaches Ohm's law, applies it to a simple series circuit, and verifies independent use on a changed circuit, directly fulfilling the accepted outcome."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "ohm_series_integrated",
            "outcomeRefs": [],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1"
            ],
            "title": "Learn, apply, and independently check Ohm's law in one simple series circuit",
            "activityType": "Integrated explanation, supported application, and independent changed example",
            "purpose": "Teach the rule, demonstrate it on a worked series circuit, and verify independent use on a changed circuit, which is the accepted stopping point.",
            "instructions": "1) Read or recall the statement of Ohm's law: V = I × R, with V in volts, I in amperes, and R in ohms. 2) In a simple series circuit with one voltage source and one resistor, the same current flows through every component and the resistor's voltage drop equals the source voltage. 3) Work through this example: a 12 V source connected to a 4 Ω resistor. Use I = V / R to find I = 12 / 4 = 3 A. Check by substituting back: V = I × R = 3 × 4 = 12 V, which matches the source. 4) Now solve this changed circuit independently, without help: a 9 V source connected to a 3 Ω resistor. Find the current, then verify your answer by substituting back into V = I × R. 5) Write one or two sentences explaining why the current is the same through the source and the resistor in a simple series circuit, and why dividing voltage by resistance gives current. 6) If your independent answer or explanation is wrong, identify the specific error (for example, using R = V / I instead of I = V / R, or mixing units) and correct it before stopping.",
            "completionCriterion": "You correctly solve the changed 9 V / 3 Ω circuit (I = 3 A), verify it by substitution, and explain in your own words why current is the same through the series components and why I = V / R applies.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 25,
              "likelyMinutes": 35,
              "highMinutes": 50
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 35,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": []
          },
          {
            "localId": "ohm_series_remediation",
            "outcomeRefs": [],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1"
            ],
            "title": "Targeted remediation for Ohm's law in a simple series circuit",
            "activityType": "Failure-triggered targeted remediation",
            "purpose": "Provide bounded additional explanation and one new changed circuit only if the independent check in the committed activity fails.",
            "instructions": "Only do this if the independent changed circuit in the committed activity was incorrect or the explanation was missing. 1) Re-read the definition of each quantity and the correct rearrangement: I = V / R, V = I × R, R = V / I. 2) Re-work the original 12 V / 4 Ω example slowly, saying aloud why each step is valid. 3) Solve one new changed circuit: a 6 V source with a 2 Ω resistor. Find the current and verify by substitution. 4) Write one sentence explaining why the current is the same through the source and resistor in a simple series circuit. Stop after this single new circuit is correct and explained.",
            "completionCriterion": "The new 6 V / 2 Ω circuit is solved correctly (I = 3 A), verified by substitution, and the series-current explanation is correct.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 15,
              "likelyMinutes": 20,
              "highMinutes": 30
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 20,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "CONTINGENT",
              "trigger": {
                "checkpointActivityId": "ohm_series_integrated",
                "condition": "The independent changed circuit (9 V / 3 Ω) is solved incorrectly, or the required explanation of why current is the same through series components and why I = V / R applies is missing or wrong."
              }
            },
            "hardDependencies": [
              "ohm_series_integrated"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [
            "The learner has no prior demonstrated readiness for Ohm's law in series circuits, so the activity starts from the rule itself.",
            "No verified assessment weighting or deadline exists, so effort is sized to the accepted outcome rather than to an exam.",
            "The learner's preferred timebox of 30 minutes is a soft preference; the committed activity is set at 35 minutes because it integrates explanation, application, and an independent check."
          ],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "No separate practice set or second full check is scheduled because the accepted outcome explicitly stops after one independent changed circuit; adding more would exceed the requested scope.",
            "No timed exam technique or paper analysis is included because there is no verified assessment or deadline."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "The learner's current familiarity with rearranging V = I × R is unknown; if the committed activity reveals a gap, the contingent remediation addresses it.",
            "No assessment weighting is known, so no topic is prioritized by exam importance."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop after the committed integrated activity if the changed circuit is solved correctly and the explanation is adequate.",
            "If the committed activity fails, complete the contingent remediation once and then stop."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "The accepted outcome is intentionally small and has no deadline or verified assessment weighting, so the plan uses one committed integrated activity that teaches, applies, and independently checks Ohm's law in a simple series circuit. A second full practice set or separate check is not scheduled because it would exceed the requested stopping point; instead, a single failure-triggered remediation is available only if the independent changed circuit fails. This keeps the plan honest to the requested scope while still providing a bounded path if the check reveals a gap."
}

