            ],
            "title": "Select and apply circuit methods on unfamiliar circuits",
            "activityType": "Integrated method-selection practice",
            "purpose": "Make the method-selection rule explicit, apply it to a worked unfamiliar circuit, then solve a changed unfamiliar circuit independently.",
            "instructions": "1) Write the selection rule you will use: for each circuit, inspect topology and source type, then choose the method whose assumptions actually apply (for example, node-voltage when a common reference node and unknown node voltages are natural; mesh-current when independent loops are natural; superposition when multiple independent sources and linearity make it efficient; Thevenin/Norton when only one branch's behavior is needed). 2) Work one unfamiliar circuit: state the chosen method, give the selection reason from the circuit's actual topology/assumptions, solve it, and check the result by an independent route (for example, power balance or a second method on a simple case). 3) Then solve one changed unfamiliar circuit without assistance: state the chosen method, give the selection reason, solve it, and check it. 4) If your changed-circuit solution is wrong or your selection reason does not match the circuit's actual assumptions, correct that specific error and re-solve only that circuit.",
            "completionCriterion": "On the changed unfamiliar circuit, you choose a method whose assumptions/topology apply, give a valid selection reason, and obtain the correct solution without assistance; any actual error is corrected.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 45,
              "likelyMinutes": 60,
              "highMinutes": 90
            },
